# Per-suite wall-clock ceilings, one line per suite: `<suite> <seconds>` (suite name without tests/ and .sh).
# Read at runtime by bin/test-affected and tests/run-all.sh (one source). An unlisted suite gets 300.
# TEST_AFFECTED_TIMEOUT_SECS and RUN_ALL_TIMEOUT_SECS still override every suite.
#
# Rule: seconds = max(60, 2 x p95 wall time). Measured as three full parallel runs of
#   KIT_RUN_ALL=1 RUN_ALL_TIMEOUT_SECS=1200 bash tests/run-all.sh --all --time
# on one 14-core macOS arm64 host (four suites at a time, whole-second resolution) from a clone of
# master 202b00af. Three samples per suite, so p95 is the max of the three (a fourth run was
# stopped to save time; the fifth was never started). All three runs passed every suite.
# Host load average (1 minute) at the start of each run: 3.05, 26.05, 228.46. The host was
# shared with other sessions and the last run was far above normal load, so each p95 is a
# conservative upper bound and these ceilings are generous. Re-measure on a quiet host to tighten.
# Sub-second suites measure 0 s and take the 60 s floor.
# One hand-set exception: test-wrap-land caches passed sections, so its three samples (76, 1, 1) are one
# cold and two warm runs. A later cold run on a loaded host ran past 152 s and was killed, so it
# holds 600 instead of 2 x 76. Re-measure it cold (LAND_CACHE=0) before tightening.
test-lanes-data 692
test-meta-docs-registry 682
test-hooks 660
test-wrap-land 600  # hand-set: a cold run passed 152 s on a loaded host; re-measure with LAND_CACHE=0 before tightening
